The history shows the best result came from joint path selection that minimizes **marginal (deduplicated) union cost** across destinations (Attempt 1: 624), and Steiner-tree approximation came close (Attempt 2: 663). The current single-Dijkstra-tree solution (1035) regressed because it forces every destination onto its individually-cheapest path even when a slightly more expensive shared route would avoid paying for the same edges twice.

New approach — **k-shortest-path candidates + greedy marginal-cost selection with restarts + swap polish**:

1. For each destination, generate several candidate paths (`nx.shortest_simple_paths`, k-best by cost).
2. Greedily assign each destination (trying multiple random destination orderings) the candidate path that adds the **least marginal cost** to the union of already-selected edges — this deduplicates shared transfer segments and exploits multi-network parallel routes.
3. Polish: repeatedly try swapping one destination's path for an alternative candidate if it lowers total union cost.
4. Keep the best assignment across all restarts. All paths remain contiguous node paths, guaranteeing validity.

This differs fundamentally from the Dijkstra-tree approach (which uses only one candidate per dst) and from Steiner growth (no explicit candidate pool + marginal selection).

